WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.080
It's the busiest shopping day of the year.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:09.839
You tap your card at a café, and the reader thinks for four seconds before it beeps.

3
00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:15.887
Behind it, one database machine records the card payments of thousands of shops.

4
00:00:15.887 --> 00:00:20.372
It can't store the 25,000 payments a second that are arriving.

5
00:00:20.372 --> 00:00:23.048
And its disk fills up by 40 GB a day.

6
00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:25.120
A copy won't help this time.

7
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:32.998
Every copy stores every payment and writes every payment, so a fourth copy is one more full disk that writes no faster.

8
00:00:32.998 --> 00:00:36.705
So we cut the payments into parts, one machine per part.

9
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.924
Each part is called a shard.

10
00:00:38.924 --> 00:00:41.371
Every payment lives in exactly one.

11
00:00:41.371 --> 00:00:43.259
The question is: which one?

12
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:47.346
Picture a parcel depot with four bays and a van for each.

13
00:00:47.346 --> 00:00:48.751
It sorts by postcode.

14
00:00:48.751 --> 00:00:56.311
Bay 0 takes the postcodes from the start of the list up to one boundary, bay 1 takes the next stretch, and so on.

15
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:03.964
A driver who delivers to one street finds all its parcels in one bay, because neighbouring postcodes sit together.

16
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.381
Our store does the same with keys.

17
00:01:06.381 --> 00:01:08.883
A key is the value we look a row up by.

18
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:14.015
Keep the keys in sorted order, and give each shard one unbroken stretch of them.

19
00:01:14.015 --> 00:01:15.683
That's key-range sharding.

20
00:01:15.933 --> 00:01:16.957
Now pick the key.

21
00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:19.304
Say it's the time the payment was made.

22
00:01:19.304 --> 00:01:24.721
The boundaries were cut from the morning's payments, so the four shards are the same size.

23
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:26.665
It's the afternoon.

24
00:01:26.665 --> 00:01:30.241
A payment arrives, made at 14:00:00.005.

25
00:01:30.241 --> 00:01:32.566
Which shard does it go to?

26
00:01:32.566 --> 00:01:33.996
Pause and guess.

27
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:37.743
Shard 3.

28
00:01:37.743 --> 00:01:42.973
It's later than every key already stored, so it sorts past the last boundary.

29
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.736
And the next payment?

30
00:01:44.736 --> 00:01:45.506
Same story.

31
00:01:45.506 --> 00:01:49.076
All afternoon, every new payment is the newest one.

32
00:01:49.076 --> 00:01:53.555
Ten thousand new payments, and all ten thousand land on shard 3.

33
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.498
Four machines, and one does all the writing.

34
00:01:56.498 --> 00:01:59.319
The cluster writes no faster than one machine.

35
00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:03.121
A shard with far more work than the rest is called a hot spot.

36
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:07.571
The obvious fix: shard 3 is too busy, so split it in half.

37
00:02:07.571 --> 00:02:11.517
The store does exactly that, at its middle key, 14:25.

38
00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:13.124
Let's see if it helps.

39
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:15.440
Now there are five shards.

40
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.550
Shard 4 holds everything from 14:25 on.

41
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.102
And the next new payment goes...

42
00:02:21.102 --> 00:02:21.979
to shard 4.

43
00:02:21.979 --> 00:02:23.015
And the next.

44
00:02:23.015 --> 00:02:24.052
And the next.

45
00:02:24.333 --> 00:02:26.988
Splitting moved the hot spot to the end.

46
00:02:26.988 --> 00:02:28.449
It couldn't spread it.

47
00:02:28.449 --> 00:02:28.913
Well...

48
00:02:28.913 --> 00:02:32.829
we've built a brand new shard whose only job is the future.

49
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.729
Here the depot picture needs one change.

50
00:02:35.729 --> 00:02:42.429
Sort parcels by the date on the label, and every new parcel is today's, so every one goes to the last bay.

51
00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.591
This one hides well in testing.

52
00:02:44.591 --> 00:02:51.116
Load a year of old payments in one go, and they spread nicely, because the boundaries were cut to fit them.

53
00:02:51.116 --> 00:02:55.691
The trouble starts with live traffic, when every payment is the newest one.

54
00:02:55.967 --> 00:02:58.547
The fix is to put something before the time.

55
00:02:58.547 --> 00:03:02.593
Make the key the shop's id, then the time, then the payment's number.

56
00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:05.103
Now the boundaries are shop ids.

57
00:03:05.103 --> 00:03:12.651
And the afternoon's payments come from all 500 shops, so they land in all the shops' ranges, on every shard.

58
00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.621
And one shop's payments still sit together, sorted by time.

59
00:03:17.621 --> 00:03:22.343
All of shop M-0042's day is one unbroken run, on one shard.

60
00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:26.374
But there's a bill, and it arrives on the other question.

61
00:03:26.374 --> 00:03:29.848
Every payment from 14:00 to 14:05, across all shops.

62
00:03:29.848 --> 00:03:32.720
With the time first, that sat on one shard.

63
00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:37.995
With the shop first, those payments are a small run inside every shop's range.

64
00:03:37.995 --> 00:03:40.767
So the question has to ask all four shards.

65
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.528
So neither key is better.

66
00:03:42.528 --> 00:03:45.095
Each one makes a different question cheap.

67
00:03:45.095 --> 00:03:49.802
Here's the rule: whatever comes first in the key decides where new rows land.

68
00:03:49.802 --> 00:03:52.675
Choose it from the question that must be cheap.

69
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:53.977
One honest limit.

70
00:03:53.977 --> 00:03:59.615
The shop-first key spreads new payments only because they happen to come from many shops.

71
00:03:59.615 --> 00:04:03.480
Next, we'll spread keys whatever they look like, with a hash.

72
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.887
So, where do the new payments go?

73
00:04:05.887 --> 00:04:09.381
With key ranges, wherever the start of the key sends them.

74
00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:12.091
Time first, and that's always the last shard.

75
00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:13.534
One quick check.

76
00:04:13.534 --> 00:04:20.008
A shop keeps its orders in ranges of the order number, which counts up by one for each new order.

77
00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:21.943
Where do today's orders land?

78
00:04:21.943 --> 00:04:23.011
Pause and think.

79
00:04:26.233 --> 00:04:27.736
All on the last shard.

80
00:04:27.736 --> 00:04:30.946
A number that only grows is a time in disguise.

81
00:04:30.946 --> 00:04:32.653
Put something else first.
